WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.490
Welcome to a discussion
about the music

00:00:02.490 --> 00:00:04.350
for Hedwig and the Angry Inch.

00:00:04.350 --> 00:00:07.170
Stephen Trask is a Grammy
Award-nominated composer,

00:00:07.170 --> 00:00:09.630
musician, and
lyricist who composed

00:00:09.630 --> 00:00:11.730
the music and lyrics
for the musical Hedwig

00:00:11.730 --> 00:00:16.050
and the Angry Inch and the
subsequent film adaptation.

00:00:16.050 --> 00:00:20.040
His other credits for film
include The Station Agent,

00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:24.270
In Good Company, American
Dreamz, and Lovelace.

00:00:24.270 --> 00:00:26.530
Stephen, thanks for joining us.

00:00:26.530 --> 00:00:28.890
We're really excited
to talk to you.

00:00:28.890 --> 00:00:32.220
Music is such an important
part of this project

00:00:32.220 --> 00:00:35.440
and we'd love to hear a little
bit about the development.

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:39.540
Let's talk about adapting
your music and songs

00:00:39.540 --> 00:00:42.070
from the Broadway musical
for the big screen.

00:00:42.070 --> 00:00:45.870
One thing I definitely
knew I wanted to do was--

00:00:45.870 --> 00:00:48.540
the look on the
screen was going to be

00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:53.970
much more vivid and up close
than we had on the stage.

00:00:53.970 --> 00:00:56.970
And the stage costumes
when they were up close,

00:00:56.970 --> 00:00:59.460
they were serious but there
was also a jokey side to them.

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:01.920
But in the movie that
was a serious rock band

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:03.600
and I felt like
something I wanted

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:05.730
to do in the movie was
the music really needed

00:01:05.730 --> 00:01:09.180
to sound like music that was
made by the people that looked

00:01:09.180 --> 00:01:13.530
like that and put together
a really killer band that

00:01:13.530 --> 00:01:17.910
was centered on Bob
Mould as a guitar player.

00:01:17.910 --> 00:01:21.600
Who was one of my idols from
Hüsker Dü and then Sugar.

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:25.980
Who had become a fan of the
show and lived down the street

00:01:25.980 --> 00:01:28.020
from the Jane Street Theater.

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:32.810
And we hung out
pretty regularly,

00:01:32.810 --> 00:01:36.260
like he came by saw the
end of the show a lot.

00:01:36.260 --> 00:01:37.840
He saw the show
a lot in general,

00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:41.330
brought a lot of musicians
that I respected there.

00:01:41.330 --> 00:01:44.060
And then he just hung out, we
became really close friends,

00:01:44.060 --> 00:01:47.000
and I ended up dog
sitting for him a lot,

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:49.910
which was convenient because
it was right near the theater.

00:01:49.910 --> 00:01:54.520
And then he was he was the
centerpiece of the recording

00:01:54.520 --> 00:01:55.210
band.

00:01:55.210 --> 00:01:58.870
So I tried to produce it with
a slightly less polished sound

00:01:58.870 --> 00:02:03.430
than the cast album had
and just a little more raw.

00:02:03.430 --> 00:02:07.210
And we're going to be talking
to Alex, but Alex Steyermark,

00:02:07.210 --> 00:02:09.970
he led us through the
process of figuring out

00:02:09.970 --> 00:02:15.370
how to get the sound of the
band coming out on the stage

00:02:15.370 --> 00:02:19.630
while shooting so that the
extras could see what it--

00:02:19.630 --> 00:02:21.310
could feel like they
were at a concert.

00:02:21.310 --> 00:02:22.935
People that played
the musicians worked

00:02:22.935 --> 00:02:27.160
really hard to learn their
parts and worked very closely

00:02:27.160 --> 00:02:28.660
with the actual musicians.

00:02:28.660 --> 00:02:30.880
We put on these
little fake concerts

00:02:30.880 --> 00:02:32.860
on the set for the extras.

00:02:32.860 --> 00:02:35.590
There was a lot of production
involved both on the day

00:02:35.590 --> 00:02:37.240
and then leading up to it.

00:02:37.240 --> 00:02:39.550
Besides giving all the
players their parts,

00:02:39.550 --> 00:02:43.420
we would pre-record John, do
a master take of his vocal,

00:02:43.420 --> 00:02:46.270
we would comp what we
thought was the ideal vocal,

00:02:46.270 --> 00:02:47.620
and he would learn that.

00:02:47.620 --> 00:02:51.910
When he sang live he was
singing having studied what--

00:02:51.910 --> 00:02:53.110
that master take.

00:02:53.110 --> 00:02:56.513
And then he would sing about
four or five times live

00:02:56.513 --> 00:02:58.930
and then start lip syncing,
because how many times can you

00:02:58.930 --> 00:03:01.270
sing and then still act a scene.

00:03:01.270 --> 00:03:03.400
I'm not sure how much
of what we actually

00:03:03.400 --> 00:03:06.970
did playing-wise in the movie
actually made it to the film.

00:03:06.970 --> 00:03:11.320
But that drummer man,
Michael Aronov, he was just--

00:03:11.320 --> 00:03:12.730
he hit every drum fill.

00:03:12.730 --> 00:03:16.120
He made one mistake and
it haunts him to this day.

00:03:16.120 --> 00:03:17.950
One cymbal he forgot to hit.

00:03:17.950 --> 00:03:19.730
This project is very
interesting to me

00:03:19.730 --> 00:03:24.040
because it started out in
such a simple form, such

00:03:24.040 --> 00:03:27.430
a very pure form and
then kept expanding

00:03:27.430 --> 00:03:32.740
from this kind of cabaret
or personal appearance act

00:03:32.740 --> 00:03:35.320
and then moving
into more of a play

00:03:35.320 --> 00:03:37.610
and then moving
more into the movie.

00:03:37.610 --> 00:03:42.430
So I'm just curious, what
was the feeling of Hedwig

00:03:42.430 --> 00:03:44.530
for you as the composer?

00:03:44.530 --> 00:03:46.270
As you were
composing these songs

00:03:46.270 --> 00:03:49.510
and working on this
project over those years,

00:03:49.510 --> 00:03:51.940
what was the feeling
of that character

00:03:51.940 --> 00:03:55.630
that you musically
feel was consistent?

00:03:55.630 --> 00:03:57.370
Like one of the
things that Hedwig

00:03:57.370 --> 00:03:59.950
has that is sort of like--

00:03:59.950 --> 00:04:02.980
some of it just comes
from me, and it's

00:04:02.980 --> 00:04:05.110
inspired by a lot of
the same musicians,

00:04:05.110 --> 00:04:07.450
but it's sort of an eclecticism.

00:04:07.450 --> 00:04:10.310
I listen to a lot of
different kinds of music.

00:04:10.310 --> 00:04:12.850
The songwriters that I
idolize listen to a lot

00:04:12.850 --> 00:04:14.080
of different kinds of music.

00:04:14.080 --> 00:04:17.140
When Hansel puts his head
in the oven early on,

00:04:17.140 --> 00:04:19.029
it's Anne-Marie,
and Debby Boone,

00:04:19.029 --> 00:04:23.200
and David Bowie, and
Iggy Pop, and Lou Reed.

00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:27.070
It's not just the punk and
glam and avant garde stuff

00:04:27.070 --> 00:04:30.610
that Hedwig liked, it was
an eclectic mix of things.

00:04:30.610 --> 00:04:34.720
I would name chicks like
Patti Smith and Yoko Ono

00:04:34.720 --> 00:04:38.500
but also Aretha Franklin
and also LaVern Baker.

00:04:38.500 --> 00:04:41.380
Hedwig is a student of
rock music, you know,

00:04:41.380 --> 00:04:45.130
and even to the point
of the essay that

00:04:45.130 --> 00:04:49.450
gets Hansel thrown
out of University

00:04:49.450 --> 00:04:51.730
is about the aggressive
influence of rock

00:04:51.730 --> 00:04:53.277
and roll music and--

00:04:53.277 --> 00:04:55.360
German philosophy on rock
and roll music and "You,

00:04:55.360 --> 00:04:56.693
Kant, Always Get What You Want."

00:04:56.693 --> 00:05:04.910
So this is something that
character was obsessed with

00:05:04.910 --> 00:05:09.190
and I think that the
music is drawing--

00:05:09.190 --> 00:05:12.790
however it's
expressed, it's drawing

00:05:12.790 --> 00:05:15.825
on a lot of different
sources all at the same time.

00:05:15.825 --> 00:05:17.200
And I think that's
why I'm trying

00:05:17.200 --> 00:05:20.440
to translate it to the film.

00:05:20.440 --> 00:05:25.090
It drew on all those
sources but it had a more--

00:05:25.090 --> 00:05:29.360
the sound is more start
to finish cohesive than

00:05:29.360 --> 00:05:32.650
in the original Off-Broadway
show where it felt like--

00:05:32.650 --> 00:05:36.057
where it jumped genres a lot.

00:05:36.057 --> 00:05:37.890
We got away with it
because we were on stage

00:05:37.890 --> 00:05:39.480
and we were wearing
those costumes.

00:05:39.480 --> 00:05:44.550
So this was your first feature,
and was this the first exposure

00:05:44.550 --> 00:05:46.590
you had to filmmaking
in this way,

00:05:46.590 --> 00:05:48.480
where you were a
major part of it...

00:05:48.480 --> 00:05:50.560
Like in a professional sense?

00:05:50.560 --> 00:05:51.510
Absolutely.

00:05:51.510 --> 00:05:56.580
I did a-- did a movie
musical in college that

00:05:56.580 --> 00:06:02.560
was a thesis film
for Miguel Arteta

00:06:02.560 --> 00:06:06.210
and it won all sorts of
awards, like film festivals,

00:06:06.210 --> 00:06:07.620
like Berlin Film Festival.

00:06:07.620 --> 00:06:10.650
And I think it got a
Student Academy Award.

00:06:10.650 --> 00:06:13.080
It was a similar sort of thing
where we actually had to--

00:06:13.080 --> 00:06:16.140
like wrote the songs,
recorded the songs,

00:06:16.140 --> 00:06:19.750
taught them to the actors,
performed them on the screen.

00:06:19.750 --> 00:06:21.780
But it was obviously
for no money

00:06:21.780 --> 00:06:24.040
and with student equipment.

00:06:24.040 --> 00:06:25.950
But for what it was,
it was celebrated.

00:06:25.950 --> 00:06:30.000
And I know they had a copy
of it on "Buffy the Vampire

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:34.813
Slayer" set and they watched it
a lot according to Joss Whedon.

00:06:34.813 --> 00:06:35.480
That's terrific.

00:06:35.480 --> 00:06:39.270
So it had its influence.

00:06:39.270 --> 00:06:40.230
That's amazing.

00:06:40.230 --> 00:06:45.750
So what was the transition
like for you to go into this?

00:06:45.750 --> 00:06:47.610
Even though it was
an independent film

00:06:47.610 --> 00:06:52.770
it was definitely, as you say,
a professional, major release

00:06:52.770 --> 00:06:53.500
film.

00:06:53.500 --> 00:06:56.160
You know I think I was--

00:06:56.160 --> 00:06:57.750
I wasn't a kid.

00:06:57.750 --> 00:07:01.800
I did this show for a year
at the Jane Street Theater.

00:07:01.800 --> 00:07:05.310
And during that time we did
all sorts of appearances

00:07:05.310 --> 00:07:07.165
and we recorded an
album and we tried

00:07:07.165 --> 00:07:08.790
to launch the show
in some other cities

00:07:08.790 --> 00:07:13.240
and it didn't work because
the productions were unwieldy.

00:07:13.240 --> 00:07:15.450
So by that time I was
just sort of like,

00:07:15.450 --> 00:07:17.640
it was just sort of like
walking into a new room

00:07:17.640 --> 00:07:19.140
of a house for me.

00:07:19.140 --> 00:07:22.140
And with definitely different
responsibilities but it

00:07:22.140 --> 00:07:24.990
didn't feel like Oh my
God what's this new thing.

00:07:24.990 --> 00:07:26.340
It was like Oh this--

00:07:26.340 --> 00:07:30.960
it was just very exciting
new ways of doing the thing,

00:07:30.960 --> 00:07:32.700
if that makes sense.

00:07:32.700 --> 00:07:33.450
Completely.

00:07:33.450 --> 00:07:36.420
I do want to bring Alex
into the conversation now,

00:07:36.420 --> 00:07:41.400
so Alex Steyermark has worked
as a music supervisor, editor,

00:07:41.400 --> 00:07:45.600
and producer since 1998
on projects as varied

00:07:45.600 --> 00:07:48.660
as The Boxer, The Ice Storm,
and, of course, Hedwig

00:07:48.660 --> 00:07:49.920
and the Angry Inch.

00:07:49.920 --> 00:07:53.730
His feature film directorial
debut had a musical kick

00:07:53.730 --> 00:07:54.540
to it---

00:07:54.540 --> 00:07:56.520
Prey for Rock &amp; Roll.

00:07:56.520 --> 00:07:58.800
Please welcome Alex Steyermark.

00:07:58.800 --> 00:08:00.782
Alex come on board.

00:08:00.782 --> 00:08:01.960
Hello.

00:08:01.960 --> 00:08:04.860
So I think the first
thing, you know,

00:08:04.860 --> 00:08:07.890
music supervisor is
one of those titles

00:08:07.890 --> 00:08:11.280
that I think people think
they have an idea of what it's

00:08:11.280 --> 00:08:13.350
like to supervise
music, but I don't

00:08:13.350 --> 00:08:17.110
think they really know what that
entails on a day-to-day basis.

00:08:17.110 --> 00:08:19.980
So could you two talk about
your working relationship

00:08:19.980 --> 00:08:23.680
and how the composer and
music supervisor work together

00:08:23.680 --> 00:08:27.330
to get a cohesive sound
and cohesive feel?

00:08:27.330 --> 00:08:31.590
Well, I think the job,
the music supervisor

00:08:31.590 --> 00:08:35.679
is something that is different
depending on the film.

00:08:35.679 --> 00:08:38.909
And I think the needs of a
movie dictate what that is.

00:08:38.909 --> 00:08:42.179
And I also think that different
people bring different skill

00:08:42.179 --> 00:08:43.440
sets to it.

00:08:43.440 --> 00:08:48.197
For me, it's that I had studied
film but also studied music

00:08:48.197 --> 00:08:48.780
my whole life.

00:08:48.780 --> 00:08:52.350
So I always came
at it from the sort

00:08:52.350 --> 00:08:56.250
of filmmaker's side with
a sensitivity, I guess,

00:08:56.250 --> 00:08:58.590
to how music is created.

00:08:58.590 --> 00:09:02.230
On this particular film,
which was a massive actually

00:09:02.230 --> 00:09:04.770
a massive endeavor given
the amount of music

00:09:04.770 --> 00:09:08.040
in the film and the fact that
both John and Stephen were

00:09:08.040 --> 00:09:09.510
making their first film.

00:09:09.510 --> 00:09:13.950
It was a very complex
project and I had sort of

00:09:13.950 --> 00:09:18.900
become known as someone who
was good with on-camera films

00:09:18.900 --> 00:09:20.810
with on-camera
performances as well.

00:09:20.810 --> 00:09:25.140
So I was brought
in by, originally,

00:09:25.140 --> 00:09:28.080
by the producer of the
film, Christine Vachon,

00:09:28.080 --> 00:09:32.890
to meet and talk with Stephen
and John about the film.

00:09:32.890 --> 00:09:38.160
It also helped that I had a lot
of music production experience.

00:09:38.160 --> 00:09:41.040
Also I had seen the show
at the Jane Street Theater

00:09:41.040 --> 00:09:46.890
so what seemed to be one of
the challenges of the film

00:09:46.890 --> 00:09:52.560
was to translate the intensity
of what it was like to actually

00:09:52.560 --> 00:09:55.530
be in the theater
and see and feel

00:09:55.530 --> 00:09:58.890
viscerally the music that
you felt as an audience

00:09:58.890 --> 00:10:02.190
member sitting in a
theater with live music.

00:10:02.190 --> 00:10:06.120
And that was sort of a guiding
principle throughout and how we

00:10:06.120 --> 00:10:07.770
approached filming the scenes.

00:10:07.770 --> 00:10:10.470
And there was-- as
Stephen said, there

00:10:10.470 --> 00:10:17.310
was a lot of actual on-set
music production taking place.

00:10:17.310 --> 00:10:25.470
Another challenge was that John
was both director and star.

00:10:25.470 --> 00:10:30.210
And so that was a very
physically demanding task

00:10:30.210 --> 00:10:32.100
that I don't even
think John really

00:10:32.100 --> 00:10:35.140
realized he was getting into.

00:10:35.140 --> 00:10:42.820
And so it was a matter of
recreating that intensity

00:10:42.820 --> 00:10:45.920
on film of the live show.

00:10:45.920 --> 00:10:50.330
It was also having it--
sort of, organizing

00:10:50.330 --> 00:10:52.910
the filming and the
production of the music

00:10:52.910 --> 00:10:56.150
around physical
limitations also budget,

00:10:56.150 --> 00:10:59.030
it was not a big budget film.

00:10:59.030 --> 00:11:03.440
And we had to be fairly
resourceful in how we did

00:11:03.440 --> 00:11:05.680
that, how we approached that.

00:11:05.680 --> 00:11:09.110
Stephen, when Alex
came on board what

00:11:09.110 --> 00:11:14.310
was that kind of getting to
know each other process like?

00:11:14.310 --> 00:11:17.330
The best part of the
getting to know you process

00:11:17.330 --> 00:11:22.370
was, in addition to recording
the songs, which all happened

00:11:22.370 --> 00:11:24.530
before the shooting,
I scored the film,

00:11:24.530 --> 00:11:27.230
I wrote the instrumental score
that went throughout the film

00:11:27.230 --> 00:11:28.790
but I had no equipment.

00:11:28.790 --> 00:11:33.200
I had a four track machine
and I had a TV with a VCR.

00:11:33.200 --> 00:11:35.930
I don't think I even had a
remote control for my TV,

00:11:35.930 --> 00:11:39.080
so like the idea of pressing
play and pressing record

00:11:39.080 --> 00:11:41.450
and then getting to pick up
my instrument with my one

00:11:41.450 --> 00:11:46.520
microphone-- it just it was
trying to write music for it.

00:11:46.520 --> 00:11:49.220
I could write the music but
I couldn't write it really

00:11:49.220 --> 00:11:50.370
for it.

00:11:50.370 --> 00:11:58.250
And so I actually wrote all of
the demo cues in Alex's office

00:11:58.250 --> 00:12:02.780
with Alex recording me while
I would watch the movie there.

00:12:02.780 --> 00:12:05.030
And sometimes write
it there in the room.

00:12:05.030 --> 00:12:07.010
You know it's weird
to think about now

00:12:07.010 --> 00:12:11.300
because like I can do
almost anything with scoring

00:12:11.300 --> 00:12:11.990
in a computer.

00:12:11.990 --> 00:12:14.570
But Alex had what
I think at the time

00:12:14.570 --> 00:12:19.880
was very sophisticated computer
technology for putting the--

00:12:19.880 --> 00:12:24.290
editing the music and
sticking it into the picture

00:12:24.290 --> 00:12:27.260
but looking back on it
was very, very primitive

00:12:27.260 --> 00:12:28.300
sort of technology.

00:12:28.300 --> 00:12:29.750
Right Alex?

00:12:29.750 --> 00:12:32.420
Certainly by today's
standards, it was primitive.

00:12:32.420 --> 00:12:33.930
But we had a lot of fun with it.

00:12:33.930 --> 00:12:37.790
It was appropriate for the
circumstances in a way.

00:12:37.790 --> 00:12:39.620
You had to be pretty inventive.

00:12:39.620 --> 00:12:40.260
And it was.

00:12:40.260 --> 00:12:42.135
And it was-- I just
remember working really--

00:12:42.135 --> 00:12:43.340
being there after hours.

00:12:43.340 --> 00:12:45.680
We were in your office
till really late at night.

00:12:45.680 --> 00:12:47.540
We were, and when
it came to the score

00:12:47.540 --> 00:12:49.730
there was also a question of--

00:12:49.730 --> 00:12:53.000
we're getting away from the
actual songs and everything

00:12:53.000 --> 00:12:55.225
but the score is a big
part of it actually.

00:12:55.225 --> 00:12:57.310
Even though there's
not a lot of it,

00:12:57.310 --> 00:12:59.360
it plays a very important role.

00:12:59.360 --> 00:13:03.440
And we were searching for
what that's actually--

00:13:03.440 --> 00:13:06.120
what the thematic
material should be.

00:13:06.120 --> 00:13:09.680
And at a certain
point, it dawned on us

00:13:09.680 --> 00:13:13.520
that really we should
take the melodic material

00:13:13.520 --> 00:13:16.250
from a song that was
already in the film

00:13:16.250 --> 00:13:21.800
and use that as the theme,
the thematic material

00:13:21.800 --> 00:13:23.550
for the score.

00:13:23.550 --> 00:13:25.010
So then when we
get to the end it's

00:13:25.010 --> 00:13:27.450
just this tremendous
cathartic release,

00:13:27.450 --> 00:13:29.630
which I think you're
not completely

00:13:29.630 --> 00:13:33.570
aware of as an audience
member but it's there.

00:13:33.570 --> 00:13:37.160
We're so focused on the song
and that was beautiful actually.

00:13:37.160 --> 00:13:38.990
The world was just so--

00:13:38.990 --> 00:13:42.800
was so tight and coherent
that it didn't really--

00:13:42.800 --> 00:13:44.630
it wasn't looking
for new material.

00:13:44.630 --> 00:13:47.250
It wasn't looking
for a new song,

00:13:47.250 --> 00:13:49.190
it wasn't looking
for new anything.

00:13:49.190 --> 00:13:55.250
It just wanted to be fleshed
out and so like picking up

00:13:55.250 --> 00:13:59.390
on themes that were already in
the songs, particularly "Origin

00:13:59.390 --> 00:14:03.095
Of Love," and using
that in a few spots.

00:14:03.095 --> 00:14:06.380
I think the flying
music is unique unto

00:14:06.380 --> 00:14:09.410
itself and it had to bridge--

00:14:09.410 --> 00:14:15.260
it had to somehow be score
but like the flying music had

00:14:15.260 --> 00:14:19.130
to somehow feel like it was
in the same musical world

00:14:19.130 --> 00:14:22.550
as the "Angry Inch"
which was fading out

00:14:22.550 --> 00:14:25.190
and the beginning
of "Wig in a Box"

00:14:25.190 --> 00:14:28.460
which when the flying
music is over you're there.

00:14:28.460 --> 00:14:30.680
Basically it bridges
those two worlds

00:14:30.680 --> 00:14:35.290
and it had to feel like
it was in that space

00:14:35.290 --> 00:14:40.540
without being a song.

00:14:40.540 --> 00:14:44.490
And like now when
I score a film,

00:14:44.490 --> 00:14:46.810
I actually like I
know how to do that.

00:14:46.810 --> 00:14:48.320
Like it's a very-- like it--

00:14:48.320 --> 00:14:52.060
say someone like gives me a
film with a lot of songs in it,

00:14:52.060 --> 00:14:57.550
I know how to figure out how
to do stuff that can go in

00:14:57.550 --> 00:15:00.190
between the songs and it's
not really that big a deal

00:15:00.190 --> 00:15:04.580
but then it felt like a
really heavy lift for me.

00:15:04.580 --> 00:15:10.090
And I guess like
the other was like--

00:15:10.090 --> 00:15:12.820
what-- you did-- you did that
live performance in a Spike Lee

00:15:12.820 --> 00:15:14.500
movie right, Alex?

00:15:14.500 --> 00:15:16.060
Sure, many of them, yeah.

00:15:16.060 --> 00:15:21.160
I love Spike Lee and I
loved all those movies.

00:15:21.160 --> 00:15:23.710
So one, I just felt
like I was in good hands

00:15:23.710 --> 00:15:25.490
when we were
introduced and when you

00:15:25.490 --> 00:15:27.680
were brought into our world.

00:15:27.680 --> 00:15:30.460
But also feeling like knowing
that we were doing it right

00:15:30.460 --> 00:15:33.610
and figuring out
that way of mixing

00:15:33.610 --> 00:15:37.030
live performance and
pre-recorded performance

00:15:37.030 --> 00:15:44.500
and mimicked live performance
on the set like that

00:15:44.500 --> 00:15:47.710
was the thing that you
taught us how to do.

00:15:47.710 --> 00:15:48.430
And so--

00:15:48.430 --> 00:15:52.040
I've always been, especially
with this kind of music,

00:15:52.040 --> 00:15:56.770
I've always believed
pretty strongly that it's--

00:15:56.770 --> 00:15:58.420
to the extent that
it can be done,

00:15:58.420 --> 00:16:02.440
it's important to
do live vocals.

00:16:02.440 --> 00:16:06.070
It's more believable when
you see it coming out

00:16:06.070 --> 00:16:09.160
of the actor's mouth.

00:16:09.160 --> 00:16:14.230
We had the fortunate
situation that John

00:16:14.230 --> 00:16:18.250
was both a great actor
and a great singer.

00:16:18.250 --> 00:16:20.950
Those basic ingredients
were already there.

00:16:20.950 --> 00:16:24.190
Initially the
producers of the film

00:16:24.190 --> 00:16:27.070
were wondering why
we couldn't just

00:16:27.070 --> 00:16:32.020
do lip-sync, which is what most
people were doing certainly

00:16:32.020 --> 00:16:33.050
at the time.

00:16:33.050 --> 00:16:36.100
And for me, it was
just clear that again,

00:16:36.100 --> 00:16:39.040
that if we were going to
recreate the energy that we

00:16:39.040 --> 00:16:42.730
felt in the theater and if we
were going to believe that what

00:16:42.730 --> 00:16:46.180
we were seeing on screen
was really happening

00:16:46.180 --> 00:16:47.770
we had to do it
with live vocals.

00:16:47.770 --> 00:16:50.650
And so with that being
said, as Stephen mentioned,

00:16:50.650 --> 00:16:54.010
we pre-recorded everything,
all of the instrumental tracks,

00:16:54.010 --> 00:16:59.420
we pre-recorded John's vocals,
background vocals, as well.

00:16:59.420 --> 00:17:04.240
And which-- so when we
were filming the musicians

00:17:04.240 --> 00:17:08.380
were actually miming
their instruments

00:17:08.380 --> 00:17:13.000
but John was singing live
and we were playing back

00:17:13.000 --> 00:17:15.819
the instrumental
tracks in the room

00:17:15.819 --> 00:17:19.060
over loudspeakers so that
the actors could hear.

00:17:19.060 --> 00:17:21.380
We were using real
stage microphones,

00:17:21.380 --> 00:17:24.235
which are pretty good
at isolating the voice.

00:17:26.800 --> 00:17:31.510
And whenever-- I'd
say it's probably

00:17:31.510 --> 00:17:36.230
90% or more live
vocals in the film.

00:17:36.230 --> 00:17:41.110
And we use the prerecorded
vocals because John--

00:17:41.110 --> 00:17:44.140
it was just very,
very demanding.

00:17:44.140 --> 00:17:48.040
I don't know that John, I'm
sure that John didn't realize

00:17:48.040 --> 00:17:50.020
how demanding it would be.

00:17:50.020 --> 00:17:52.990
It's one thing to
go through a show

00:17:52.990 --> 00:17:55.270
from beginning to end
in a couple of hours.

00:17:55.270 --> 00:17:58.780
It's another thing to spend
eight hours in a day singing

00:17:58.780 --> 00:18:01.510
the same song over and over
again without blowing out

00:18:01.510 --> 00:18:03.050
your voice.

00:18:03.050 --> 00:18:07.120
And so whenever we
weren't filming John then

00:18:07.120 --> 00:18:09.580
we would be playing back the
prerecorded vocals, which

00:18:09.580 --> 00:18:14.950
is what the extras or the
audience in the scenes

00:18:14.950 --> 00:18:20.440
would be responding to or
what Stephen and the rest

00:18:20.440 --> 00:18:23.140
of the band would be miming to.

00:18:23.140 --> 00:18:26.490
The band did play
everything note perfect.

00:18:26.490 --> 00:18:27.340
Yes.

00:18:27.340 --> 00:18:29.832
I remember the drummer had--

00:18:29.832 --> 00:18:31.290
coming out of the
drummer's monitor

00:18:31.290 --> 00:18:34.360
was the drummers part
loud enough so that--

00:18:34.360 --> 00:18:37.330
for the audience the
sound of the drums

00:18:37.330 --> 00:18:40.000
seemed to really actually
be coming from the drums.

00:18:40.000 --> 00:18:42.700
And that was something that
made it feel more live.

00:18:42.700 --> 00:18:44.920
Every day it felt like a live--

00:18:44.920 --> 00:18:48.130
it felt like a musical
production unto itself.

00:18:48.130 --> 00:18:50.080
I'm pretty sure that
a lot of the extras

00:18:50.080 --> 00:18:52.030
actually thought they
were hearing live music

00:18:52.030 --> 00:18:54.310
like entirely live performance.

00:18:54.310 --> 00:18:56.665
They mentioned that,
they were like, "wow,

00:18:56.665 --> 00:18:59.260
you guys played great".

00:18:59.260 --> 00:19:01.780
We were like, uh-huh, thank you.

00:19:01.780 --> 00:19:05.710
What's one of your favorite
moments musically in the film?

00:19:05.710 --> 00:19:07.860
Well, here's one.

00:19:07.860 --> 00:19:11.110
With me singing Tommy's version
of 'Wicked Little Town.'

00:19:11.110 --> 00:19:13.810
Something really nice
happened in that recording.

00:19:13.810 --> 00:19:16.960
I remember Bob coming up
with an amazing guitar,

00:19:16.960 --> 00:19:20.080
like did something to the
guitar part that just brought it

00:19:20.080 --> 00:19:21.310
to a new level.

00:19:21.310 --> 00:19:22.270
But it wasn't just Bob.

00:19:22.270 --> 00:19:25.570
And Tim O'Heir was recording
it and he got a great sound.

00:19:25.570 --> 00:19:27.910
And everyone played
really well and something

00:19:27.910 --> 00:19:29.470
about that recording.

00:19:29.470 --> 00:19:31.317
And then when sung
by Michael Pitt who

00:19:31.317 --> 00:19:33.210
has that beautiful face.

00:19:33.210 --> 00:19:36.990
Not sung by him, but he's
lip syncing to me singing.

00:19:36.990 --> 00:19:43.330
It just-- I don't know,
that one recording

00:19:43.330 --> 00:19:44.950
has a very special sound to it.

00:19:44.950 --> 00:19:47.080
I remember that,
actually, when we did that

00:19:47.080 --> 00:19:49.090
and it's very vivid.

00:19:49.090 --> 00:19:54.280
You were so in another place,
as a performer you were really

00:19:54.280 --> 00:19:55.390
feeling the part.

00:19:55.390 --> 00:19:58.510
I remember that it was like you
were acting it in the studio

00:19:58.510 --> 00:20:00.610
and I think that's
why it feels that way.

00:20:00.610 --> 00:20:03.220
That makes sense because
I also had to method act

00:20:03.220 --> 00:20:04.480
the writing of the song.

00:20:04.480 --> 00:20:08.050
So singing it was an
extension of that.

00:20:08.050 --> 00:20:11.300
Like just to write it, I
had to be somebody else.

00:20:11.300 --> 00:20:14.700
And so, yeah, I remember
being in the studio

00:20:14.700 --> 00:20:19.660
and feeling that, going and
being that other person.

00:20:19.660 --> 00:20:22.060
And then it just came
out really special.

00:20:22.060 --> 00:20:24.910
How was it determined
that you were going

00:20:24.910 --> 00:20:27.100
to sing that role for Tommy?

00:20:27.100 --> 00:20:29.930
I demanded it.

00:20:29.930 --> 00:20:33.470
What was there about that that
made you want to demand it?

00:20:33.470 --> 00:20:35.880
I knew I could sing it right.

00:20:35.880 --> 00:20:39.630
And you know we had done
the cast album with Atlantic

00:20:39.630 --> 00:20:43.290
Records and they
actually only wanted

00:20:43.290 --> 00:20:47.520
to do the soundtrack if we
let one of their big stars

00:20:47.520 --> 00:20:48.870
be Tommy Gnosis.

00:20:48.870 --> 00:20:50.370
And it would have
been Scott Weiland

00:20:50.370 --> 00:20:52.860
and he's like, you know he would
have sung the shit out of it,

00:20:52.860 --> 00:20:54.860
but it definitely would
have been Scott Weiland.

00:20:54.860 --> 00:20:57.812
Like it wouldn't have
been Tommy Gnosis singing,

00:20:57.812 --> 00:20:59.520
it would have been
Scott Weiland singing.

00:20:59.520 --> 00:21:00.840
Michael Pitt could
have mouthed it,

00:21:00.840 --> 00:21:02.160
but you'd have know who it was.

00:21:02.160 --> 00:21:04.650
And I wanted to do it,
so I just kind of like--

00:21:04.650 --> 00:21:07.050
we were very much--
when we shot this movie

00:21:07.050 --> 00:21:11.258
we took a lot of
meetings and gave

00:21:11.258 --> 00:21:13.800
the pitch of what we wanted to
do and how we wanted to do it.

00:21:13.800 --> 00:21:15.480
And it was written
into the contract

00:21:15.480 --> 00:21:19.210
that my band was
going to be the band.

00:21:19.210 --> 00:21:20.760
My band broke up,
but they were going

00:21:20.760 --> 00:21:24.000
to be the band in the movie
and on the recordings.

00:21:24.000 --> 00:21:26.460
And if there were
any disagreements

00:21:26.460 --> 00:21:29.610
about musical elements,
the film company

00:21:29.610 --> 00:21:32.520
could weigh in, but that
I had the tie breaking

00:21:32.520 --> 00:21:36.240
vote over the director
and the film studio.

00:21:36.240 --> 00:21:38.940
Like these weird--
we just demanded--

00:21:38.940 --> 00:21:43.200
like John demanded stuff, we
demanded stuff and then New

00:21:43.200 --> 00:21:46.170
Line was willing to give it
to us and Atlantic wasn't.

00:21:46.170 --> 00:21:47.760
And we ended up selling the--

00:21:47.760 --> 00:21:54.270
Alex sold the soundtrack later
to Hybrid Records that got

00:21:54.270 --> 00:21:58.080
lost I think in a card game.

00:21:58.080 --> 00:21:58.980
And--

00:21:58.980 --> 00:21:59.813
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:21:59.813 --> 00:22:01.470
Something like that, right?

00:22:01.470 --> 00:22:03.850
Well, we have to find out.

00:22:03.850 --> 00:22:06.300
But we would go around
and give our little pitch

00:22:06.300 --> 00:22:09.420
to all these film
studios and they

00:22:09.420 --> 00:22:12.540
would just kind of like look at
us like, "who are these people,

00:22:12.540 --> 00:22:14.040
they're crazy".

00:22:14.040 --> 00:22:20.070
Like we'd talk about doing this
sort of improv John Cassavetes

00:22:20.070 --> 00:22:23.930
thing where there's a lot
of not talking and whatever.

00:22:23.930 --> 00:22:27.540
And sort of like what I'm doing
right now and they would just

00:22:27.540 --> 00:22:33.330
be like, "uh-huh", really
awkward lunches and then--

00:22:33.330 --> 00:22:37.370
until finally New Line
was actually interested.

00:22:37.370 --> 00:22:39.790
It found the right home.

00:22:39.790 --> 00:22:40.480
Yeah.

00:22:40.480 --> 00:22:46.750
So Alex, going back to the
beginning of being brought on

00:22:46.750 --> 00:22:50.440
to kind of create
this film music sound

00:22:50.440 --> 00:22:53.320
and at the same time ending
up selling the soundtrack.

00:22:53.320 --> 00:22:58.030
I mean it sounds like you had
quite an interesting journey

00:22:58.030 --> 00:22:58.780
with this film.

00:22:58.780 --> 00:23:02.710
I'm just wondering what are
some of the pieces along the way

00:23:02.710 --> 00:23:05.680
that you found particularly
personal for you?

00:23:05.680 --> 00:23:08.570
Well, first of all, I loved
the show to begin with.

00:23:08.570 --> 00:23:12.380
So when I was asked to work
on the film I was thrilled.

00:23:12.380 --> 00:23:14.620
I didn't know John or
Stephen at the time.

00:23:14.620 --> 00:23:17.005
It was very complex and it got--

00:23:17.005 --> 00:23:20.650
it was one of those projects
where I got to do everything

00:23:20.650 --> 00:23:22.610
that I like to do.

00:23:22.610 --> 00:23:26.830
Which is, frankly, be involved
in creating the music as

00:23:26.830 --> 00:23:30.130
opposed to some
of the other roles

00:23:30.130 --> 00:23:33.720
that music supervisors
often play like clearances

00:23:33.720 --> 00:23:35.360
and licensing and so on.

00:23:35.360 --> 00:23:38.600
So for me I loved
that aspect of it,

00:23:38.600 --> 00:23:40.715
I've always preferred
that part of it.

00:23:40.715 --> 00:23:42.340
The greatest thing
that came out of it,

00:23:42.340 --> 00:23:45.320
frankly, was getting
to know Stephen

00:23:45.320 --> 00:23:47.760
and becoming close
friends with him.

00:23:47.760 --> 00:23:51.400
Stephen, in fact, produced the
music for my directorial debut

00:23:51.400 --> 00:23:53.390
a couple of years later,
PRAY FOR ROCK &amp; ROLL,

00:23:53.390 --> 00:23:54.223
which you mentioned.

00:23:54.223 --> 00:23:56.800
So if you're lucky, you get
to work on a lot of films

00:23:56.800 --> 00:23:59.080
and meet people,
but it's not often

00:23:59.080 --> 00:24:03.850
that you actually meet someone
that you just feel so--

00:24:03.850 --> 00:24:07.850
such a rapport with
that extends beyond just

00:24:07.850 --> 00:24:10.400
the working experience
that you have together.

00:24:10.400 --> 00:24:13.780
So at the same time it's a
great film, it's a lot of fun.

00:24:13.780 --> 00:24:16.660
I like watching it with other
people who are discovering it

00:24:16.660 --> 00:24:20.680
themselves for the first
time and seeing that it still

00:24:20.680 --> 00:24:22.150
resonates with people.

00:24:22.150 --> 00:24:26.110
And it's a big success in
terms of what we set out to do.

00:24:26.110 --> 00:24:29.320
We did create a movie that,
for lack of a better word,

00:24:29.320 --> 00:24:31.450
kicks ass musically.

00:24:31.450 --> 00:24:36.640
And that's what we wanted to do,
you know and I think it does--

00:24:36.640 --> 00:24:37.900
definitely does that.

00:24:37.900 --> 00:24:41.680
Kicks ass is definitely
I think the proper phrase

00:24:41.680 --> 00:24:42.820
for this film.

00:24:42.820 --> 00:24:46.510
Was Hedwig a hit
right away or was it

00:24:46.510 --> 00:24:50.380
something that you really had
to work to keep this project

00:24:50.380 --> 00:24:51.170
moving?

00:24:51.170 --> 00:24:52.960
At what point did
it start to get

00:24:52.960 --> 00:24:54.550
a little momentum of its own?

00:24:54.550 --> 00:24:59.220
We always were scraping by even
when we seemed to be a hit.

00:24:59.220 --> 00:25:02.020
It was always a heavy
boulder rolling up a hill.

00:25:02.020 --> 00:25:05.680
Even on Broadway, like
Neil's run was sold out

00:25:05.680 --> 00:25:09.370
but when the run started his
first night was sold out,

00:25:09.370 --> 00:25:11.140
but the second
night was half sold.

00:25:11.140 --> 00:25:13.780
It wasn't until the
first show ended

00:25:13.780 --> 00:25:16.790
and the word got out that
he was actually really good

00:25:16.790 --> 00:25:18.190
that tickets started selling.

00:25:18.190 --> 00:25:20.920
It always requires
a push, you actually

00:25:20.920 --> 00:25:22.690
can't let it just go
of its own momentum

00:25:22.690 --> 00:25:24.340
because if you
don't stay involved

00:25:24.340 --> 00:25:26.868
it kind of loses whatever
is special about it.

00:25:26.868 --> 00:25:28.660
It shouldn't feel like
a piece of commerce,

00:25:28.660 --> 00:25:32.080
it should feel like a piece
of queer, punk theater.

00:25:32.080 --> 00:25:36.190
And the queer and the punk
parts have to both be there.

00:25:36.190 --> 00:25:39.310
Like it can't just be queer
and it can't just be punk,

00:25:39.310 --> 00:25:42.130
it's queer, punk theater.

00:25:42.130 --> 00:25:45.460
And that like--

00:25:45.460 --> 00:25:49.960
John and I are queer punks and
like not everybody else is.

00:25:49.960 --> 00:25:53.980
So we have to keep our
fingers in the pie.

00:25:53.980 --> 00:25:58.360
So can you talk about,
again, this project

00:25:58.360 --> 00:26:01.150
started as kind of
just a drag performance

00:26:01.150 --> 00:26:04.360
in a very small
space and then moved

00:26:04.360 --> 00:26:06.070
to more of a theatrical venue.

00:26:06.070 --> 00:26:12.070
Can you talk about how Hedwig
changed in order to do that?

00:26:12.070 --> 00:26:15.550
The big element that
came along was getting--

00:26:15.550 --> 00:26:19.060
sort of organizing philosophy
for how to present it.

00:26:19.060 --> 00:26:22.030
Which is, the show is
always happening wherever

00:26:22.030 --> 00:26:24.140
the show happens to be.

00:26:24.140 --> 00:26:29.590
And so like when we were at--
when we did it on Broadway,

00:26:29.590 --> 00:26:31.660
the place that the
show was happening

00:26:31.660 --> 00:26:33.130
was the Belasco Theater.

00:26:33.130 --> 00:26:35.830
And Hedwig talked about
being at the Belasco Theater.

00:26:35.830 --> 00:26:37.610
And then there's
the question, well,

00:26:37.610 --> 00:26:40.240
how is this total
nobody who was a failure

00:26:40.240 --> 00:26:41.890
get onto a Broadway stage?

00:26:41.890 --> 00:26:44.590
Well, then that
becomes part of--

00:26:44.590 --> 00:26:47.620
that becomes part of
the story that she--

00:26:47.620 --> 00:26:51.790
in that case a show had closed.

00:26:51.790 --> 00:26:56.545
A show had closed on opening
night, "Hurt Locker -

00:26:56.545 --> 00:27:00.280
the Musical", which is a
name that Michael made up.

00:27:00.280 --> 00:27:06.850
And their set was still there
and Hedwig gives a blowjob

00:27:06.850 --> 00:27:09.160
to the guy that runs the
theater-- is actually

00:27:09.160 --> 00:27:11.500
the guy that runs the whole
Schubert Organization.

00:27:11.500 --> 00:27:15.700
And he gave her the theater
for just this one night.

00:27:15.700 --> 00:27:19.920
But like some of it was
just like learning new ways

00:27:19.920 --> 00:27:21.210
to kind of do the same thing.

00:27:21.210 --> 00:27:25.530
Like when we first did
it at Fez under Time Cafe

00:27:25.530 --> 00:27:28.980
and we had recordings of
Tommy Gnosis off stage.

00:27:28.980 --> 00:27:32.310
Like we didn't have a budget
so John came over to my house

00:27:32.310 --> 00:27:36.720
and we hooked a microphone up
to a guitar amp with reverb so

00:27:36.720 --> 00:27:38.830
he could sound like
he was in the stadium.

00:27:38.830 --> 00:27:42.660
And then I put that on one
channel of a four track

00:27:42.660 --> 00:27:46.890
and then on the other channels
I recorded little snippets

00:27:46.890 --> 00:27:50.820
of crowd noise from The
Concert for Bangladesh

00:27:50.820 --> 00:27:52.430
on tracks 2, 3, and 4.

00:27:52.430 --> 00:27:55.200
And they were never
really long enough to keep

00:27:55.200 --> 00:27:59.100
going so I would just keep
cross-fading from 2 to 3 to 4,

00:27:59.100 --> 00:28:02.520
to 2 to 3 to 4 to, 2,
3, to 4 so that Tommy

00:28:02.520 --> 00:28:04.290
could be talking
and saying his stuff

00:28:04.290 --> 00:28:06.420
and the crowd could
be responding.

00:28:06.420 --> 00:28:09.160
It was a makeshift version
of what we ended up doing.

00:28:09.160 --> 00:28:11.010
But it was that
philosophy of being

00:28:11.010 --> 00:28:14.068
where you are that we found
was the thing that worked.

00:28:14.068 --> 00:28:15.360
Does that answer your question?

00:28:15.360 --> 00:28:18.780
Very much so and
it's not the answer

00:28:18.780 --> 00:28:20.880
I would have expected
so thank you very much.

00:28:20.880 --> 00:28:24.540
You mentioned earlier that you
were both queer and both punk

00:28:24.540 --> 00:28:25.230
and all that.

00:28:25.230 --> 00:28:30.000
And obviously you had your music
sensibility and your music side

00:28:30.000 --> 00:28:30.690
of the story.

00:28:30.690 --> 00:28:35.170
And John was more the
performer and writer, perhaps.

00:28:35.170 --> 00:28:37.350
But what do you
see as the things

00:28:37.350 --> 00:28:41.760
that you and John brought
to this that gave it

00:28:41.760 --> 00:28:45.690
the cohesiveness and that gave
it the yin and the yang, so

00:28:45.690 --> 00:28:46.710
to speak?

00:28:46.710 --> 00:28:50.340
I kind of write half the words
that come out of Hedwig's mouth

00:28:50.340 --> 00:28:51.877
and John writes the
other words that

00:28:51.877 --> 00:28:53.460
come out of Hedwig's
mouth, but it all

00:28:53.460 --> 00:28:55.260
has to sound like Hedwig.

00:28:55.260 --> 00:28:59.580
So we had to be very
much of one mind.

00:28:59.580 --> 00:29:05.070
And so a lot of the work--

00:29:05.070 --> 00:29:08.610
it came out of
long conversations.

00:29:08.610 --> 00:29:11.460
Like, just long
days and nights just

00:29:11.460 --> 00:29:14.430
spent talking about
the various issues that

00:29:14.430 --> 00:29:17.640
related to the show.

00:29:17.640 --> 00:29:24.330
And really getting into
dualities and binaries

00:29:24.330 --> 00:29:27.030
and breaking those open.

00:29:27.030 --> 00:29:29.940
Looking at a lot of philosophy
and religious philosophy

00:29:29.940 --> 00:29:32.370
and all of this stuff and
just long conversations.

00:29:32.370 --> 00:29:34.350
So that when you
went to write, it

00:29:34.350 --> 00:29:36.720
wasn't necessarily
that you went to write

00:29:36.720 --> 00:29:41.040
with an instruction for what was
going to happen in the story,

00:29:41.040 --> 00:29:44.310
but you're writing from the
perspective of coming out

00:29:44.310 --> 00:29:47.160
of these conversations
where the two of us

00:29:47.160 --> 00:29:49.230
were just engaged in this thing.

00:29:49.230 --> 00:29:52.203
What we ended up writing was
just us talking to each other.

00:29:52.203 --> 00:29:54.120
Talking to each other
in a way that we thought

00:29:54.120 --> 00:29:55.470
would entertain our friends.

00:29:55.470 --> 00:29:57.360
In the back of our
mind, it was some day

00:29:57.360 --> 00:30:01.540
going to be something
else, but I think for me,

00:30:01.540 --> 00:30:04.750
and I'm pretty sure for John,
like the most immediate thing

00:30:04.750 --> 00:30:07.210
was we're writing to
each other and we're

00:30:07.210 --> 00:30:10.870
going to show it to our
friends who know us.

00:30:10.870 --> 00:30:16.330
And I think that's why it has
that feeling of yin and yang

00:30:16.330 --> 00:30:18.650
like totally come together.

00:30:18.650 --> 00:30:21.470
Alex, you observed
them working together

00:30:21.470 --> 00:30:24.340
and observed the
making of the film.

00:30:24.340 --> 00:30:26.500
What are the different
pieces that they each bring

00:30:26.500 --> 00:30:28.750
to the mix, so to speak?

00:30:28.750 --> 00:30:32.980
I knew a lot about the creative,
the collaborative process,

00:30:32.980 --> 00:30:37.420
how the show had been created
even before I met them.

00:30:37.420 --> 00:30:44.740
And I knew that it
would be super stressful

00:30:44.740 --> 00:30:46.470
for both of them.

00:30:46.470 --> 00:30:49.150
And so because it
was their first film,

00:30:49.150 --> 00:30:53.650
because film is usually a
director's medium, you know.

00:30:53.650 --> 00:30:56.500
And here we had
a situation where

00:30:56.500 --> 00:30:59.560
we had two creative
partners who were

00:30:59.560 --> 00:31:03.010
both equally important to
the success of the show

00:31:03.010 --> 00:31:05.500
and of the concept.

00:31:05.500 --> 00:31:09.010
In this case, also Stephen
was a creative collaborator

00:31:09.010 --> 00:31:10.520
in the making of the film.

00:31:10.520 --> 00:31:14.710
And so that was also different
and usually the music creator

00:31:14.710 --> 00:31:16.760
is not in that position.

00:31:16.760 --> 00:31:19.780
So I was always very
mindful, I was always

00:31:19.780 --> 00:31:23.620
trying to be respectful
of both John and Stephen's

00:31:23.620 --> 00:31:26.320
equal partnership in
the creation of Hedwig,

00:31:26.320 --> 00:31:27.370
the idea of Hedwig.

00:31:27.370 --> 00:31:29.980
Part of my role, besides all the
other things we talked about,

00:31:29.980 --> 00:31:33.883
was sort of navigating
the creative process

00:31:33.883 --> 00:31:35.800
that each one of them
would have to go through

00:31:35.800 --> 00:31:37.330
to create the movie.

00:31:37.330 --> 00:31:40.150
So that each of them could
do what they had to do.

00:31:40.150 --> 00:31:41.830
Honestly, I spent
a lot of time just

00:31:41.830 --> 00:31:43.810
trying to understand
where each of them

00:31:43.810 --> 00:31:47.140
was coming from so
that we could guide,

00:31:47.140 --> 00:31:52.240
from the musical part of
it, that process forward.

00:31:52.240 --> 00:31:55.008
I think it's a testament
to their relationship.

00:31:55.008 --> 00:31:57.550
They made the film and then they
went on and did the Broadway

00:31:57.550 --> 00:31:59.170
show, they have--

00:31:59.170 --> 00:32:03.820
this is a very special entity,
John and Stephen and Hedwig.

00:32:03.820 --> 00:32:05.410
This world that they've created.

00:32:05.410 --> 00:32:08.740
One of the things that was
different about the movie

00:32:08.740 --> 00:32:13.600
was that our spheres stopped
happening in the same space.

00:32:13.600 --> 00:32:15.430
Recording all the
music for the film

00:32:15.430 --> 00:32:16.810
and then turning
it into an album

00:32:16.810 --> 00:32:20.680
and then recording the score,
it was this entirely other

00:32:20.680 --> 00:32:24.277
production that had
to fit with the film,

00:32:24.277 --> 00:32:26.110
but they were happening
in different spaces.

00:32:26.110 --> 00:32:29.380
And John and I really
didn't like see each other.

00:32:29.380 --> 00:32:32.050
Whereas, when you're
doing it on stage,

00:32:32.050 --> 00:32:34.750
it was me and John and
Peter Askin and Miriam--

00:32:34.750 --> 00:32:36.970
and I would have a
piano and a guitar

00:32:36.970 --> 00:32:38.950
and we would just be
sitting around a table

00:32:38.950 --> 00:32:41.660
and just performing it there.

00:32:41.660 --> 00:32:44.380
And then you get onto the
stage and the band is there

00:32:44.380 --> 00:32:47.360
and you're all working
together in the same room.

00:32:47.360 --> 00:32:51.100
But we kind of like, kind of
went off into our own spheres.

00:32:51.100 --> 00:32:53.740
And it's true with
any musical film,

00:32:53.740 --> 00:32:55.970
it's actually more
than one production.

00:32:55.970 --> 00:32:59.410
You have the film production,
but before all of that happens

00:32:59.410 --> 00:33:03.760
you have maybe a couple of
months of the music production.

00:33:03.760 --> 00:33:08.020
You're rehearsing first the
band that you have to prerecord,

00:33:08.020 --> 00:33:11.140
and John who was
singing, then you

00:33:11.140 --> 00:33:15.370
have to rehearse the actors who
are going to be in the film.

00:33:15.370 --> 00:33:19.330
And all of this has to happen
even before you can roll film.

00:33:19.330 --> 00:33:21.790
And then afterwards,
the film is done

00:33:21.790 --> 00:33:25.270
and then we did go
and do all the album

00:33:25.270 --> 00:33:29.050
mixes which was like a
whole other production.

00:33:29.050 --> 00:33:31.060
There were a lot
of moments where--

00:33:31.060 --> 00:33:34.690
the what was happening
in the music sphere

00:33:34.690 --> 00:33:38.290
was separate from what
was happening in the film.

00:33:38.290 --> 00:33:41.570
What's your favorite
moment in the film

00:33:41.570 --> 00:33:44.690
or some of your favorite
memories from the shoot?

00:33:44.690 --> 00:33:47.780
One, I loved doing the
improvisational scenes

00:33:47.780 --> 00:33:48.570
that got cut.

00:33:48.570 --> 00:33:50.060
But there were all
sorts of things

00:33:50.060 --> 00:33:52.190
that we did that
sometimes just ended up

00:33:52.190 --> 00:33:55.910
being like an overhead
shot in the hotel room.

00:33:55.910 --> 00:33:58.580
Therese had this
great thing where

00:33:58.580 --> 00:34:02.420
in addition to touring around
the same fish restaurant,

00:34:02.420 --> 00:34:07.040
her idea was that we
were in the same hotel--

00:34:07.040 --> 00:34:09.679
motel chain throughout the tour.

00:34:09.679 --> 00:34:13.400
So the four walls were
always the same four walls.

00:34:13.400 --> 00:34:15.960
But as the tour went on,
we had less and less money,

00:34:15.960 --> 00:34:18.330
our room kept getting smaller.

00:34:18.330 --> 00:34:20.420
So at the beginning
of the tour, we're

00:34:20.420 --> 00:34:22.969
in a kind of spacious room and
we're all kind of spread out.

00:34:22.969 --> 00:34:27.201
And at the end of the tour, the
room is the width of the bed

00:34:27.201 --> 00:34:28.909
and then there's just
like a little place

00:34:28.909 --> 00:34:31.969
to stand that's just wide enough
to open the door to get in.

00:34:31.969 --> 00:34:35.900
So it's the same decor that just
kept getting closer and closer.

00:34:35.900 --> 00:34:38.239
My memory of it is
that it was a blur.

00:34:38.239 --> 00:34:43.100
It was a very tight schedule
and there was so much to do.

00:34:43.100 --> 00:34:48.560
It was non-stop activity
from morning to night.

00:34:48.560 --> 00:34:50.750
A lot of it was, to
be honest, was also

00:34:50.750 --> 00:34:54.469
getting John to pace himself
so that he wouldn't burn out

00:34:54.469 --> 00:34:56.520
because he would
throw himself into it.

00:34:56.520 --> 00:34:59.300
And again, he was
directing so he

00:34:59.300 --> 00:35:02.540
was looking at the
monitor in costume,

00:35:02.540 --> 00:35:06.620
you know, and then having to
run out and sing and perform.

00:35:06.620 --> 00:35:08.840
To be honest, I was
a little concerned.

00:35:08.840 --> 00:35:11.480
He had to make it to
the end so a lot of it

00:35:11.480 --> 00:35:17.120
was just getting him to not use
up all his energy in one moment

00:35:17.120 --> 00:35:19.400
because he had a lot to do.

00:35:19.400 --> 00:35:22.430
Filming 'Midnight Radio' was
pretty intense, you know.

00:35:22.430 --> 00:35:26.330
It was a puzzle, actually,
of how to put it together.

00:35:26.330 --> 00:35:29.060
I still kind of
feel like this jolt

00:35:29.060 --> 00:35:30.560
when I see that in the film.

00:35:30.560 --> 00:35:33.020
Especially when we
cut to Miriam falling

00:35:33.020 --> 00:35:35.180
back and the whole thing.

00:35:35.180 --> 00:35:38.210
Alex was mentioning like getting
John to pace himself and how

00:35:38.210 --> 00:35:41.990
much work he was doing-- on the
visual side like Therese was

00:35:41.990 --> 00:35:43.150
like--

00:35:43.150 --> 00:35:45.700
of all the people
on the visual side,

00:35:45.700 --> 00:35:47.920
she was the most
experienced, but I

00:35:47.920 --> 00:35:54.010
think Alex probably brought the
most film experience of anybody

00:35:54.010 --> 00:35:55.330
to this movie.

00:35:55.330 --> 00:35:57.190
You know, I could feel
that when we first

00:35:57.190 --> 00:35:58.720
started working together.

00:35:58.720 --> 00:36:01.210
And it was very important
to me, actually Alex,

00:36:01.210 --> 00:36:04.730
to either impress you
or to get your approval,

00:36:04.730 --> 00:36:07.320
but like that sense
of Alex has done this,

00:36:07.320 --> 00:36:11.440
he knows what this is-- like
I felt in very good hands.

00:36:11.440 --> 00:36:13.810
And bringing you into
the world-- like it

00:36:13.810 --> 00:36:16.370
felt right to bring
you into there.

00:36:16.370 --> 00:36:17.900
I'm glad you brought me into it.

00:36:17.900 --> 00:36:18.700
Thank you.

00:36:18.700 --> 00:36:21.340
Yeah, me too.

00:36:21.340 --> 00:36:25.300
Stephen, Alex, I want to thank
you both for this conversation.

00:36:25.300 --> 00:36:29.770
It's really great to talk
about your collaboration

00:36:29.770 --> 00:36:31.420
on this film with the music.

00:36:31.420 --> 00:36:34.870
And it's also great to
see that you've each

00:36:34.870 --> 00:36:37.000
found a friend so
congratulations

00:36:37.000 --> 00:36:38.350
and thank you very much.

00:36:38.350 --> 00:36:39.940
Thank you.

00:36:39.940 --> 00:36:41.680
Thanks for asking me
to be a part of it.

00:36:41.680 --> 00:36:44.290
Sorry you are up at
4:00 in the morning

00:36:44.290 --> 00:36:46.792
but I don't think this
is the first time.

00:36:46.792 --> 00:36:47.292
No.

00:36:50.000 --> 00:36:52.950
It's like the good
old days, Stephen.

